						<td style="font-size: 11pt;"> 
							 
							<br>
							<br>
							06:47 AM EST March 20, 2003 
							<br>
							The Associated Press<br><br><br>SEOUL, South Korea<p class="nitfp">Facing a bench of four South Korean judges in black robes, an American exchange student accused of killing another American female student in Seoul attended a 10-minute court hearing Thursday.</p>
<p class="nitfp">Suspect Kenzi Snider, 21, wore an olive prison uniform and had her long blond hair tied into a ponytail. She faces a minimum of three years in jail if convicted on homicide charges.</p>
<p class="nitfp">Judge Kim Nam-taek said written U.S. investigation reports were inadmissible as evidence, in accordance with Korean legal methods. That means one or both of the U.S. investigators involved in the case - one from the FBI and the other from the U.S. Army - will have to testify.</p>
<p class="nitfp">U.S. investigators believe the former Marshall University student from St. Cloud, Minn., beat, kicked and trampled Jamie Lynn Penich to death in a Seoul motel room in March 2001. Snider was allegedly enraged by sexual advances from Penich.</p>
<p class="nitfp">The strategy of defense lawyer Eom Sang-ik is to undermine the allegations by the U.S. investigators, and he had requested that the written U.S. reports not be accepted as evidence.</p>
<p class="nitfp">"The U.S. investigators' reports are the pillar that supports this case. If we break them, the whole case falls," Eom said. Reports by South Korean police "found no strong suspicion against Snider," he said.</p>
<p class="nitfp">Snider, who was extradited to South Korea in December, had confessed to killing Penich during questioning by U.S. investigators in the United States. She later said she had been coerced into making the confession and believes investigators are framing her to protect two American soldiers who allegedly killed Penich.</p>
<p class="nitfp">Snider and Penich, a 21-year-old University of Pittsburgh student, danced and drank at a bar in Seoul with two GIs on the night of Penich's death. The women met while studying as exchange students at Keimyung University in southern South Korea.</p>
<p class="nitfp">At the hearing Thursday, the court's translator explained the proceedings to Snider and asked whether she agreed with her lawyer's arguments.</p>
<p class="nitfp">"I agree with him," Snider said.</p>
<p class="nitfp">Her attorney sat at a desk several yards to her right, while the prosecutor sat the same distance away on her left.</p>
<p class="nitfp">It was the second hearing since her trial opened on March 6. The court decided to call FBI agent Seungkyu Lee, a Korean American, as a witness at the next hearing on April 3.</p>
<p class="nitfp">Snider left the court room as her mother, Heath Bozonie, whispered "love you" and blew a kiss toward her daughter.</p>
<p class="nitfp">Bozonie has been traveling back and forth between South Korea and Thailand, where she teaches English and social studies at a middle school. Last week, she gave up her Thailand job to stay in Seoul until the trial ends.</p>

